WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
When a massive star exhausts one core fuel,

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:05.140
the core contracts and heats. If it gets

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.400
hot enough, a heavier fuel ignites. Earlier fuels

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.480
can keep burning in surrounding shells. Repeated cycles

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.580
build a layered interior, and the later burning

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.520
stages become much shorter. The whole star does

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.280
not burn one element everywhere.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.520
For the five elements in the class ordering

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.680
exercise, go inward through hydrogen, helium, carbon, silicon,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.340
and iron. This selected list leaves out other

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.040
layers in a detailed model. The center contains

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.740
the most processed material. Keep direction straight: reversing

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.860
the question to center outward reverses the order.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.000
Fusion toward the iron group releases energy, but

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.420
further fusion does not provide the same energy

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.240
source. Iron therefore marks the end of the

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.460
normal core fusion sequence. Silicon can still be

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.820
the heaviest correct answer when iron is absent

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.780
from the choices. Elements beyond iron require other

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.880
pathways; supernovae are not their only source.
